{
  "about": "Truth mappings for claims (kernel/speech.py truth(); reports.py truth at report time; docs/design/VERIFY_HARDENING.md 13.3, 13.6). A claim is scored only when its word has a mapping here or in a domain's data/speech/truth_<domain>.json (same sections; a key defined in two files fails at load). Conditions read the subject's own record (an entity, a room, a person or a pet): {field, op, value} with the predicate language's operators; a dotted field reads a sub-field. states: the FIRST condition whose field the record has decides (a lamp's on decides before lit). issues: ANY condition whose field the record has and that holds makes the issue true; all present and false make it false; none present leaves it unknown. Generic issues also fall back to the vocabulary's evidence fields named like the issue or one of its synonyms, when they are booleans. quantities: the record fields holding the quantity in its canonical unit (C, kg, l, m ...), first present wins; mass reads the subtree mass (what a scale shows). acts: the end state a commitment maps to (state: a state word on the subject; to_listener: the subject ends with the person spoken to; placement: the subject ends where the commitment says, \"I'll put the box in the hall\") and the core actions that perform it. A future commitment is kept when its end state holds at the end, whoever brought it about. A past first-person commitment (\"I locked the side gate\") is about the robot's own act (VERIFY_HARDENING 13.3): true when the robot did it -- one of the actions on the subject in its history, a robot write of the end state's fields on the subject in the writer log (a physical write of them that may be the robot's doing counts), or for a placement the robot's own last placement of the subject in the place --, false when the end state does not hold, and also false when it holds but the robot did not do it, for acts whose records see every way of doing them (agency strict: the default for acts with an end state on record fields, a hand-over or a placement); for acts known only by action names (check, call, wipe ...) the default is lenient: no robot act leaves the claim unscored. An act may list \"fields\": record fields of the subject whose robot write shows the act was done, and \"agency\": \"strict\" | \"lenient\" to override the default; feed is strict on the pet's serving record (served_g, written when the robot serves its bowl), so \"I fed Biscuit\" is false when the robot did not serve him (13.3), whoever else did. Mappings are conservative: a word whose truth the kernel cannot read reliably (clean, full, hot, fed) is left to the domains.",
